course: dictionary
lesson: 11567
title: Anecdotes
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: An anecdote is a short story about something that really happened to a person. People tell anecdotes to make a talk more interesting or funny. «My gran told an anecdote about the day her bike fell into the pond.» The clues are told and happened: the story is short, and it is about a real event.
explain: Use context to check your meaning. Swap your idea into the sentence: «My gran told a short story about something that really happened» still makes sense, so you are right. Anecdotal is the adjective, and it describes a story from one person's experience.
explain: «The teacher said the new game was great, but that was only anecdotal evidence.» Anecdotal evidence is one person's story. It is weaker than a proper test with many cases, because one story may not be true for everyone.
fact: An anecdote is a short story about a real event or person.
fact: Anecdote is a noun. Anecdotal is the adjective.
fact: Speakers often start with an anecdote to catch the listeners' interest.
fact: «Anecdotal evidence» means one person's story, which is weaker than a proper test with many cases.
fact: Context clue: an anecdote is about something that happened, and it is usually short and interesting or funny.
exercise: «Dad told a funny anecdote about getting lost in the supermarket.» What is an anecdote? | A long list of rules | A question you must answer | *A short story about a real event | A kind of sweet
exercise: Ben says his new trainers are fast, but his evidence is anecdotal. What does anecdotal mean here? | Based on many careful tests | Made of rubber | *Based on one person's story, not a proper test | Very loud and funny
exercise: «Grandma shared an anecdote about her first day at school.» Find and copy the word that names a short story about a real event. | grandma | *anecdote | shared | school
test: «In assembly, the head teacher began with an anecdote about a lost dog.» What did the head teacher begin with? | *A short story about a real event | A maths test | A song | A list of numbers
test: Which is weaker evidence? | A proper test with many cases | Both are equally strong | Neither can ever be used | *Anecdotal evidence, one person's story
exam: Which sentence uses anecdote correctly? | "My uncle ate an anecdote for tea." | "The anecdote was a red bicycle with wheels." | "We anecdoted the cake in the oven." | *"My uncle told an anecdote about the time he fell in the lake."
